WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.705
Installation describes the possibility that adversary-controlled software, access,

00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:07.500
or configuration becomes established after initial activity.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.695
Persistence risk should be handled carefully and defensively.

00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:14.500
Responders do not need a catalog of persistence techniques to use this stage well;

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.000
they need to know what changed, whether the change is authorized,

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.000
and whether it could allow continued access.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.500
Endpoint monitoring, application control, change detection, startup and service review,

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.000
integrity monitoring, privileged access control,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.000
and configuration baselines all help.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.995
This stage also requires humility.

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:33.000
Many modern incidents do not involve traditional malware installation.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.190
Cloud, SaaS, and identity-driven incidents may leave evidence in tokens, sessions, roles,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.331
automation rules,

00:00:39.331 --> 00:00:43.445
OAuth grants, API keys, or configuration changes rather than on a single endpoint.

00:00:43.445 --> 00:00:47.205
Analysts should avoid forcing the evidence into a malware-centered story.

00:00:47.205 --> 00:00:51.000
Instead, ask what capability or access could persist, where it would appear in telemetry,

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.495
who owns the affected system,

00:00:52.495 --> 00:00:55.495
and what containment would reduce risk while preserving evidence.

00:00:55.495 --> 00:00:59.400
Good use of this stage helps teams find unauthorized change and decide whether recovery

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.290
requires cleanup,

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.929
rebuild, revocation, or configuration correction.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:07.000
Defenders should also consider how persistence risk changes by platform.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Endpoint, server, cloud, SaaS, and identity systems all leave different evidence.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.070
A strong review looks across those layers instead of assuming that one endpoint scan can

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.890
answer every persistence question.
